<div class="box yellow">
<div class="box shadow">
<div style="text-align:left;">

<h2>
What is the General Election on November 3rd and why is it important?</h2>

<p>
Voting in a General Election is important because it allows voters to select the candidate they wish to represent them in office.</p>

<p>
In this election, voters will determine who serves as the next Governor of Arizona and positions in the state’s executive branch for the next four years, along with offices at the federal and local level, including the state legislature. Likewise, the General Election allows voters the opportunity to decide whether judges and justices of the Arizona Supreme Court and certain lower courts should be retained or replaced.</p>

<p>
Voters also have an opportunity to approve or reject laws proposed by the legislature and by voters themselves, known as referendums and initiatives. These measures are referred to by their proposition number.</p>

<p><a href="/arizona-elections/propositions" id="106" class="button yellow">Propositions</a> </p>



</div></div> </div>






<div class="box shadow">
<div style="text-align:left;">
<h2>Jurisdictional Elections</h2>

<p>
<b>Pima County</b> has been notified of the following jurisdictions holding an election on November 3, 2026. Contact the <i>jurisdiction</i> directly for more information about their election.</p>


<h3>Candidate Contests:</h3>

<p>
<ol>

<li><a href="https://www.southtucsonaz.gov/" target="_blank">City of South Tucson</a> -  Council Member (Runoff) – 2 seats</li>
<li><a href="https://drexelfire.org/" target="_blank">Drexel Heights Fire District</a> -  Board Member – 2 seats</li>
<li><a href="https://www.hiddenvalleyfd.org/" target="_blank">Hidden Valley Fire District</a> -  Board Member – 1 seat</li>
<li><a href="https://mescalj6fd.gov/" target="_blank">Mescal J6 Fire District</a> -  Board Member – 3 seats</li>
<li><a href="https://nwfdaz.gov/" target="_blank">Northwest Fire District</a> -  Board Member – 2 seats</li>
<li><a href="https://www.amphi.com/" target="_blank">Amphitheater Unified School District No. 10</a> -  Governing Board – 2 seats, Governing Board (Term Expires 12/31/2028) – 1 seat</li>
<li><a href="https://www.tanqueverdeschools.org/" target="_blank">Tanque Verde Unified School District No. 13</a> -  Governing Board – 2 seats</li>
<li><a href="https://www.susd30.us/" target="_blank">Sahuarita Unified School District No. 30</a> -  Governing Board – 2 seats</li>
<li><a href="https://pimajted.org/" target="_blank">Pima JTED District 2</a> -  Governing Board – 1 seat</li>

</ol>
</p>


<h3>Ballot Measures:</h3>

<p>
<ol>

<li><a href="https://www.tucsonaz.gov/Departments/Clerks/Elections" target="_blank">City of Tucson</a> - Prop 421 & Prop 422</li>
<li><a href="https://www.vailschooldistrict.org/" target="_blank">Vail Unified School District</a> - Prop 423</li>
<li><a href="https://nwfdaz.gov/" target="_blank">Northwest Fire District</a> -Prop 424</li>
<li><a href="https://www.pima.gov/394/" target="_blank">Pima County/Countywide</a> - Prop 425</li>
<li><a href="https://www.amphi.com/" target="_blank">Amphitheater Unified School District</a> - Prop 426</li>
<li><a href="https://pimajted.org/" target="_blank">Pima JTED</a> - Prop 427</li>
<li><a href="https://www.susd30.us/" target="_blank">Sahaurita Unified School District</a> - Prop 428</li>
<li><a href="https://www.tanqueverdeschools.org/" target="_blank">Tanque Verde Unified School District</a> - Prop 429</li>
<li><a href="https://www.pima.edu/" target="_blank">Pima Community College</a> - Prop 430</li>
<li><a href="https://www.orovalleyaz.gov/Government/Departments/Town-Clerk/Town-Election-Information" target="_blank">Town of Oro Valley</a> - Prop 431</li>

</ol>
</p>


</div></div> 








<div class="box shadow">
<div style="text-align:left;">

<h2>Ways to Vote</h2>
<span class="bright">Early Voting</span>
<p>Early voting begins on <b>October 7, 2026</b>. Voters are able to vote by mail or visit an early voting location. Pima county will have a staggered opening, with more voting locations available closer to election day. Contact your county recorder, contact information below, to request a ballot by mail. For more voting options please utilize our Voter Dashboard.<br />

</p>

<p>
<a href="https://www.recorder.pima.gov/EarlyVotingSites" target="_blank" class="button yellow">Early Voting & Ballot Drop-Off Locations</a> 
</p>

<p><a href="https://web1.pima.gov/elections/livefeed/"  target="_blank" class="button dark">Ballot Count Livestream</a></p>

<span class="bright">Voting on Election Day</span>
<p> Pima County utilizes <b>vote centers</b>. Vote Centers are voting locations available on Election Day in certain counties. Vote Centers are different than polling places in that any eligible voter can visit a vote center in their county and receive and cast their ballot. Click the button below to view Pima County's vote center locations.<br /><br />

 
<a href="https://storageccec.blob.core.usgovcloudapi.net/public/docs/1500-General_-Election_Vote_Center-List_2026-Pima.pdf" id="1500" target="_blank" class="button yellow">Voting Locations</a> 

</p>



<h2>How to Return Your Early Ballot</h2>

<p><h3>1. By Mail</h3>

<ul class="dot">
<li><b>Deadline:</b> Mail your ballot back by <b>October 27th</b>.</li>
</ul></p>


<p>
<h3>2. At a Drop-Box or Voting Location</h3>

<ul class="dot">
<li><b>Before Election Day:</b> Drop your ballot off at any official voting location or secure drop box.</li>
<li><b>On Election Day:</b> You have two options at the polls:
<ul class="dot">
<li><b>Option A (Fastest drop-off):</b> Drop your signed ballot into the secure bin. Your signature will be verified later before your ballot is counted.</li>
<li><b>Option B (Fastest processing):</b> Show a valid ID to a poll worker to verify your identity on the spot. This skips the signature verification step and speeds up how quickly your ballot is processed.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

</div></div> 







<div class="box shadow">
<div style="text-align:left;">
<h2>About the Central Arizona Water Conservation District:</h2>

<p>
The Central Arizona Water Conservation District (CAWCD) is a special purpose taxing district, referred to as a
multi-county conservation district, that is managed by a 15-member board elected from the three-county
service area made up of Maricopa, Pima, and Pinal counties. The Central Arizona Project, also known as <a href="https://www.cap-az.com/" target="_blank">CAP</a>,
is a 336-mile canal that brings water from the Colorado River to Central and Southern Arizona. This
government entity was created for the purposes of establishing the delivery of Central Arizona Project water,
repayment of CAP costs, and operation and maintenance of the CAP aqueduct. The Board members serve
alternating six year terms.</p>

<p>
In 2026, one seat from Pinal County and four seats from Pima County were up for election.</p>

<p>
In Pinal County, only one candidate qualified for the ballot. With the election uncontested, Pinal County
cancelled the 2026 CAWCD election and deemed incumbent Stephen Miller elected to the CAWCD Board on
August 26, 2026.</p>

<p>
Similarly, only four candidates qualified for the four available seats in Pima County. With the election
uncontested, Pima County cancelled the 2026 CAWCD election and deemed incumbents Karen Cesare, Pat
Jacobs, Justin Manuel and Mark Taylor elected to the CAWCD Board on August 25, 2026.</p>

<p>
Terms for all five seats will begin on January 1, 2027.</p>

<p>For additional information: <a href="https://www.cap-az.com/about/" target="_blank">https://www.cap-az.com/about/</a></p>


</div></div> 














